1
00:00:06,883 --> 00:00:11,758
In 1858 the Crown took over India from the East India Company.

2
00:00:11,871 --> 00:00:15,487
Colonial censuses counted people by religion and caste,

3
00:00:15,487 --> 00:00:18,536
and used the boxes to allot jobs and seats.

4
00:00:18,649 --> 00:00:22,568
Whether the counting hardened those boxes is still argued.

5
00:00:22,851 --> 00:00:31,045
On 23 March 1940, at Lahore, the Muslim League called for Muslim-majority areas to be grouped as independent

6
00:00:31,045 --> 00:00:31,750
states.

7
00:00:31,863 --> 00:00:34,178
What that meant is still argued.

8
00:00:34,461 --> 00:00:38,505
In 1946 the Cabinet Mission proposed a loose union,

9
00:00:38,505 --> 00:00:41,026
and by July the plan had collapsed.

10
00:00:42,809 --> 00:00:49,000
On 16 August 1946, a hartal in Calcutta became about four days of killing.

11
00:00:49,113 --> 00:00:53,633
The dead number from about 4,000 to 10,000 or more;

12
00:00:53,633 --> 00:00:55,478
no reliable count exists.

13
00:00:55,761 --> 00:00:59,982
On 3 June 1947 Mountbatten announced the plan.

14
00:01:00,095 --> 00:01:03,137
Pakistan came into being on 14 August,

15
00:01:03,137 --> 00:01:08,992
India on the 15th, and the boundary awards were published on 17 August.

16
00:01:09,275 --> 00:01:13,621
Cyril Radcliffe, a London barrister who had never been to India,

17
00:01:13,621 --> 00:01:19,368
reached Delhi on 8 July 1947 with about five weeks to draw two lines.

18
00:01:19,785 --> 00:01:22,479
He did not attend the Lahore hearings.

19
00:01:22,593 --> 00:01:26,043
The judges could not agree, so he decided alone.

20
00:01:26,357 --> 00:01:31,279
The award is dated 12 August and was published on 17 August,

21
00:01:31,279 --> 00:01:33,297
two days after independence.

22
00:01:33,611 --> 00:01:36,369
Here is the line as the map prints it.

23
00:01:36,483 --> 00:01:40,363
Radcliffe wrote that there are legitimate criticisms of it,

24
00:01:40,363 --> 00:01:43,353
as of any other line that might be chosen.

25
00:01:45,137 --> 00:01:51,045
Between about ten and twenty million people left their homes and crossed the new borders.

26
00:01:51,159 --> 00:01:53,853
The number depends on what is counted.

27
00:01:54,137 --> 00:01:56,599
How many were killed is not known.

28
00:01:56,713 --> 00:02:01,089
Published estimates run from about 200,000 to two million,

29
00:02:01,089 --> 00:02:04,105
and no census or registry counted the dead.

30
00:02:04,522 --> 00:02:09,278
Trains carrying refugees were stopped and attacked at stations in Punjab,

31
00:02:09,278 --> 00:02:10,743
in both directions.

32
00:02:11,026 --> 00:02:13,766
Survivors also recall neighbours who hid,

33
00:02:13,766 --> 00:02:17,511
fed and escorted people of other communities to safety.

34
00:02:17,794 --> 00:02:23,525
Gandhi was shot dead at Birla House in New Delhi on 30 January 1948.

35
00:02:23,941 --> 00:02:29,920
In the autumn of 1947, Delhi’s old fort and open grounds became refugee camps.

36
00:02:30,203 --> 00:02:37,374
Every evening since 1959, the two border forces have lowered their flags together at Attari-Wagah.

37
00:02:37,657 --> 00:02:40,851
In 2019 the Kartarpur Corridor opened,

38
00:02:40,851 --> 00:02:46,992
letting Indian Sikh pilgrims visit a shrine four kilometres across the border without a visa.

39
00:02:47,275 --> 00:02:55,380
Saadat Hasan Manto left Bombay for Lahore in 1948 and wrote some of the sharpest short fiction on 1947.

40
00:02:55,663 --> 00:02:59,861
The 1947 Partition Archive, begun in 2010,

41
00:02:59,861 --> 00:03:03,915
has recorded more than ten thousand interviews with survivors,

42
00:03:03,915 --> 00:03:09,134
made decades later; those who died in 1947 cannot be interviewed.

43
00:03:09,417 --> 00:03:12,185
Even the words are debated: partition,

44
00:03:12,185 --> 00:03:14,512
independence, taqsim, batwara.

45
00:03:14,625 --> 00:03:19,352
Historians ask whether 1947 was one event or a long process.
